Every diagnostic carries the specific thing to do about it. Until this was
printed, only `--format agent` showed it, so a person running `check` saw the
finding and not the fix, and the first pilot reasoned its way to disabling a
guard whose remedy the tool had already written.

It appears once per rule, not once per finding: the advice is the same for every
finding of a code, so repeating it is what would make a large report unreadable,
and withholding it is what made a small one unactionable.

```
$ aplan check
warning[APLAN009] W-01: no `effort_days` estimate
  --> aplan/tasks/W-01.md
  fix: Add an `effort_days` estimate, or drop the task.
warning[APLAN009] W-02: no `effort_days` estimate
  --> aplan/tasks/W-02.md
warning[APLAN009] W-03: no `effort_days` estimate
  --> aplan/tasks/W-03.md
(2 further finding(s) carry their own fix; `--format agent` prints every one)
ok: 3 tasks, 3 warning(s), DAG acyclic

```

`--format agent` is unchanged and still carries the instruction on every
diagnostic, because something already consumes it that way; `violations.trycmd`
pins that shape.
